const char s3618[] = "GPS_UBX_DYNMODEL";
const char s3619[] = "u-blox GPS dynamic platform model";
const char s3620[] = "u-blox receivers support different dynamic platform models to adjust the navigation engine to the expected application environment.";
const char s3621[] = "Heading (Rover With Moving Base UART1 Connected To Autopilot, UART2 Connected To Moving Base)";
const char s3622[] = "Moving Base (UART1 Connected To Autopilot, UART2 Connected To Rover)";
const char s3623[] = "Heading (Rover With Moving Base UART1 Connected to Autopilot Or Can Node At 921600)";
const char s3624[] = "Moving Base (Moving Base UART1 Connected to Autopilot Or Can Node At 921600)";
const char s3625[] = "Rover with Static Base on UART2 (similar to Default, except coming in on UART2)";
const char s3626[] = "Ground Control Station (UART2 outputs NMEA)";
const char s3627[] = "GPS_UBX_MODE";
const char s3628[] = "u-blox GPS Mode";
const char s3629[] = "Select the u-blox configuration setup. Most setups will use the default, including RTK and dual GPS without heading. If rover has RTCM corrections from a static base (or other static correction source) coming in on UART2, then select Mode 5. The Heading mode requires 2 F9P devices to be attached. The main GPS will act as rover and output heading information, whereas the secondary will act as moving base. Modes 1 and 2 require each F9P UART1 to be connected to the Autopilot. In addition, UART2 on the F9P units are connected to each other. Modes 3 and 4 only require UART1 on each F9P connected to the Autopilot or Can Node. UART RX DMA is required. RTK is still possible with this setup. Mode 6 is intended for use with a ground control station (not necessarily an RTK correction base).";
const char s3630[] = "Select the u-blox configuration setup. Most setups will use the default, including RTK and dual GPS without heading. If rover has RTCM corrections from a static base (or other static correction source) coming in on UART2, then select Mode 5. The Heading mode requires 2 F9P devices to be attached. The main GPS will act as rover and output heading information, whereas the secondary will act as moving base. Modes 1 and 2 require each F9P UART1 to be connected to the Autopilot. In addition, UART2 on the F9P units are connected to each other. Modes 3 and 4 only require UART1 on each F9P connected to the Autopilot or Can Node. UART RX DMA is required. RTK is still possible with this setup.";
const char s3631[] = "Select the u-blox configuration setup. Most setups will use the default, including RTK and dual GPS without heading. The Heading mode requires 2 F9P devices to be attached. The main GPS will act as rover and output heading information, whereas the secondary will act as moving base. Modes 1 and 2 require each F9P UART1 to be connected to the Autopilot. In addition, UART2 on the F9P units are connected to each other. Modes 3 and 4 only require UART1 on each F9P connected to the Autopilot or Can Node. UART RX DMA is required. RTK is still possible with this setup.";
const char s3632[] = "Heading";
const char s3633[] = "Select the u-blox configuration setup. Most setups will use the default, including RTK and dual GPS without heading. The Heading mode requires 2 F9P devices to be attached. The main GPS will act as rover and output heading information, whereas the secondary will act as moving base, sending RTCM on UART2 to the rover GPS. RTK is still possible with this setup.";
const char s3634[] = "GPS_YAW_OFFSET";
const char s3635[] = "Heading offset angle for dual antenna GPS setups that support heading estimation. Set this to 0 if the antennas are parallel to the forward-facing direction of the vehicle and the rover (or Unicore primary) antenna is in front. The offset angle increases clockwise. Set this to 90 if the rover (or Unicore primary, or Septentrio Mosaic Aux) antenna is placed on the right side of the vehicle and the moving base antenna is on the left side. (Note: the Unicore primary antenna is the one connected on the right as seen from the top).";
const char s3636[] = "Heading offset angle for dual antenna GPS setups that support heading estimation. Set this to 0 if the antennas are parallel to the forward-facing direction of the vehicle and the rover (or Unicore primary) antenna is in front. The offset angle increases clockwise. Set this to 90 if the rover (or Unicore primary) antenna is placed on the right side of the vehicle and the moving base antenna is on the left side. (Note: the Unicore primary antenna is the one connected on the right as seen from the top).";
const char s3637[] = "Heading offset angle for dual antenna GPS setups that support heading estimation. Set this to 0 if the antennas are parallel to the forward-facing direction of the vehicle and the rover antenna is in front. The offset angle increases clockwise. Set this to 90 if the rover antenna is placed on the right side of the vehicle and the moving base antenna is on the left side.";
const char s3638[] = "Heading offset angle for dual antenna GPS setups that support heading estimation. (currently only for the Trimble MB-Two). Set this to 0 if the antennas are parallel to the forward-facing direction of the vehicle and the first antenna is in front. The offset angle increases clockwise. Set this to 90 if the first antenna is placed on the right side and the second on the left side of the vehicle.";
